<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="023" project="spaarkeai-compose-r8">
  <metadata>
    <title>Control measurement — run the oracle on current master and publish today's real loss numbers</title>
    <phase>2 Oracle &amp; Corpus</phase>
    <gate>blocked-on-020</gate>
    <status>pending</status>
    <rigor>STANDARD</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Runs an existing harness and publishes results; no product source modified. But the interpretation gates Phase 3.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Capability-matched despite being "just run it": the deliverable is an INTERPRETATION — which losses are real, which are oracle artifacts, and whether the thresholds the spec assumed (100% near / ≥95% overall) are the right bar now that a number exists. A plausible-but-wrong reading here sets the gate at the wrong level and either passes a broken model or blocks a good one.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Depends on 020, 021 and 022 all being present; its output is the input to Phase 3.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>020, 021, 022</deps>
    <tags>testing, compose, integration-test</tags>
    <estimated-effort>4 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Run the task-020 oracle over the full extended corpus on **current master** — before any Track A change —
    and publish the result as the CONTROL.

    This is the number the whole project argues from. R6's render-on-save loses formatting silently; nobody has
    ever measured how much. Two outputs matter equally:

    1. **The numbers** — near-tier and overall preservation, at both comparison levels, per document and in
       aggregate, plus the terminal outcome distribution.
    2. **The interpretation** — which losses are genuine (the merge model must fix them) versus oracle
       artifacts (the oracle must stop reporting them). Every reported loss must be classified into one of
       those two buckets. An unclassified loss is not evidence.

    Then answer the open spec question: with a real control in hand, are 100% near-tier / ≥95% overall the
    right thresholds? The spec explicitly flags these as assumed pending this measurement.
  </prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE test architect. Expert in the Compose fidelity harness and OOXML structural diffs. Able to distinguish a genuine preservation failure from a comparison artifact by reading the diff.</role>

  <goal>
    A published control measurement at `projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/control-measurement.md`: the
    numbers, a per-loss classification (genuine vs artifact), a threshold recommendation with reasoning, and
    an explicit statement of what the merge model must achieve to clear the gate.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      The control exists to make the Phase-3 gate falsifiable. Without it, "the merge model preserves 96%"
      means nothing — it could be a large improvement or a regression. It also protects the oracle: if the
      control shows losses that turn out to be artifacts, they are fixed HERE, while nothing depends on them,
      rather than during the gate when the pressure is to loosen the bar.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="doc-output">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/control-measurement.md</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">tests/integration/seam/Compose/ComposeFidelityGateHarnessTests.cs</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">tests/fixtures/compose-corpus/corpus-manifest.md</file>
      <file role="canonical-reference">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/gate-contract.md</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">MUST run against current master's write path with NO Track A change applied. A control taken on partially-fixed code is not a control.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Every reported loss MUST be classified as GENUINE or ARTIFACT, with the evidence (the actual XML diff) cited. An unclassified loss may not be counted in either direction.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Oracle artifacts found here MUST be fixed in task 020's harness and the control RE-RUN — not annotated as known-noise. Noise in the oracle becomes noise in the gate.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">MUST NOT modify any `src/` file. If the measurement reveals a product defect, record it as a finding for the relevant task; do not fix it here.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">The threshold recommendation MUST be reasoned from the data, and MUST state what it would mean to MISS it — the spec's escalation rule (a gate miss goes to the owner) only works if the miss condition is defined in advance.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-029">If a build is required, report publish size per the standard rule.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <topic>fidelity measurement, control experiments, OOXML diffs</topic>
    <files>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/gate-contract.md</file>
      <file>docs/architecture/COMPOSE-READ-REFERENCE-FIDELITY.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/spec.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/CLAUDE.md</file>
      <file>projects/spaarkeai-compose-r7/notes/uat-issues.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Rigor declaration">Declare rigor STANDARD; load spec.md, CLAUDE.md, gate-contract.md.</step>
    <step order="1">Confirm the tree has no Track A change applied. Record the commit SHA the control is taken at.</step>
    <step order="2">Run the oracle over the full extended corpus (task 021 + 022 fixtures included). Capture per-document and aggregate numbers at both comparison levels.</step>
    <step order="3">For each reported loss, read the actual XML diff and classify GENUINE or ARTIFACT with evidence.</step>
    <step order="4">Fix any ARTIFACT in task 020's harness; re-run and record the corrected control. State clearly which numbers are pre- and post-correction.</step>
    <step order="5">Record the terminal outcome distribution — how many corpus saves reach each member of task 013's enum on master today.</step>
    <step order="6">Recommend thresholds with reasoning, and define the miss condition explicitly.</step>
    <step order="7">State what the merge model must achieve, per construct family, to clear the gate.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the control shows near-100% preservation on master, the oracle is not measuring the known R6 loss — STOP and return to task 020. Proceeding would set the gate against an oracle that cannot see the defect the project exists to fix.</trigger>
    <trigger>If the data suggests the spec's assumed thresholds are unreachable for a legitimate reason (e.g. a construct family that no merge model can preserve), that is an owner decision about scope, not a threshold to quietly lower.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <outputs>
    <output type="doc">projects/spaarkeai-compose-r8/notes/control-measurement.md</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/seam/Compose/ComposeFidelityGateHarnessTests.cs (only if artifact corrections are required)</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>The control records the exact commit SHA and confirms no Track A change was applied.</criterion>
    <criterion>Per-document and aggregate preservation numbers are published at BOTH comparison levels, near tier and overall.</criterion>
    <criterion>Every reported loss is classified GENUINE or ARTIFACT with the XML diff cited as evidence.</criterion>
    <criterion>Any ARTIFACT was fixed in the harness and the control re-run; pre- and post-correction numbers are both stated.</criterion>
    <criterion>The terminal outcome distribution across the corpus is recorded against task 013's enum.</criterion>
    <criterion>A threshold recommendation is given with reasoning from the data, and the MISS condition is defined explicitly.</criterion>
    <criterion>What the merge model must achieve per construct family is stated.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: no `src/` file was modified; any product defect found is recorded as a finding against its owning task.</criterion>
    <criterion>NEGATIVE: the control does not report ~100% preservation (which would indicate a blind oracle).</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
